{"records":[{"id":"66ms37y66x","title":"Data for: A genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screen reveals that riboflavin regulates hydrogen peroxide entry into HAP1 cells  ","description":"CRISPR screen data for hydrogen peroxide mediated cell death","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-02-13T14:30:16.414Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/66ms37y66x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/66ms37y66x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"surachai","last_name":"supattapone","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kenneth","last_name":"Mark","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tamutenda","last_name":"Chidawanyika","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Oxidative Stress"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mmnwww6n3s","title":"LZTFL1 Rewires NADPH–Glutathione Metabolism to Amplify Ferroptosis","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent form of regulated cell death, represents a therapeutic vulnerability in apoptosis-resistant cancers. Here, we identify leucine zipper transcription factor-like 1 (LZTFL1) as a key regulator of ferroptosis that rewires glutathione (GSH) metabolism. A genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screen and multi-omics analysis revealed that LZTFL1 promotes oxidation of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD), limiting NADPH production and GSH regeneration. GSH depletion in turn enhanced LZTFL1 translation via an AKT–mTOR–eIF4E pathway, establishing a feed-forward loop that amplifies ferroptosis. In vivo, the LZTFL1–FHOD1–G6PD axis sensitized xenograft and patient-derived xenograft models to ferroptosis, correlating with lipid peroxidation, reduced GSH, and extended survival. Clinically, LZTFL1 expression restored cisplatin efficacy in resistant lung and ovarian cancer cells and predicted better survival outcomes in LUAD patients. Moreover, FDA-approved compounds upregulated LZTFL1 and re-sensitized resistant tumors to cisplatin. These findings define LZTFL1 as both a biomarker and therapeutic target for enhancing ferroptosis-based cancer therapy. The files below contain raw western blot images and flow cytometry data from this study.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-12T06:59:22.445Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mmnwww6n3s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mmnwww6n3s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"XIANGFEI","last_name":"XUE","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Flowmetry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zxzgp65bx5","title":" PJ34 protects hair cells from cisplatin‐induced ototoxicity in vitro: possible relation to inhibition of PARP-1-AIF-mediated parthanatos","description":"PARP inhibitor PJ34 exerts anti-inflammatory and neuroprotective effects by regulating parthanatos. The objective of this study was to investigate the protective effects of PJ34 on auditory cells against cisplatin-induced injury as well as to explore the potential mechanisms involved in its action. Flow cytometry, immunofluorescence staining, Western blot, Mitochondrial membrane potential (MMP) assay and MitoSox red staining were employed to detect apoptosis, determine the expression levels of cleaved caspase-3, PARP-1, and AIF, measure MMP levels, and evaluate the levels of reactive oxygen species (ROS) in the corresponding HEI-OC1 auditory cells, ovarian cancer cell lines, and mouse cochlear hair cells. Our results demonstrated that exposure to 30 μM cisplatin activated cleaved caspase-3, PARP-1, and induced AIF nuclear translocation, leading to a decrease in MMP and an increase in ROS levels, which induced auditory cell death. Treatment with 2.5 μM PJ34 attenuated the hyperactivation of PARP-1 and nuclear translocation of AIF after cisplatin exposure, rescued the reduction of MMP and ROS accumulation, and thus protected auditory cells from cisplatin-induced cellular damage. Furthermore, PJ34 enhanced the sensitivity of ovarian cancer cell lines to cisplatin treatment. In conclusion, our findings suggest that PJ34 may attenuate cisplatin-induced hair cell death by regulating PARP-1-mediated parthanatos. Interestingly, PJ34 shows promise as a potential novel therapeutic agent for the prevention and/or treatment of cisplatin-induced ototoxicity. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-09T13:49:17.994Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zxzgp65bx5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zxzgp65bx5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Huiming","last_name":"Nong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiru","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yingxue","last_name":"Yuan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Junhong","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhixin","last_name":"Cao","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82201293"}],"categories":["Otorhinolaryngology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hrtj23vgfb","title":"scRNA-seq and spatial transcriptomics of MIBC across different ICB exposure and response state","description":"This dataset comprises single-cell and multi-scale spatial transcriptomic profiles generated to investigate microenvironmental regulation of tumor cell fate plasticity and ICB resistance in muscle-invasive bladder cancer (MIBC). The data include scRNA-seq from 11 tissue specimens across 6 MIBC patients covering distinct anatomical regions, alongside spatial transcriptomics consisting of 4 10x Visium HD sections and 6 high-resolution Xenium in situ profiling sections.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-12T16:09:32.509Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hrtj23vgfb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hrtj23vgfb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"泽涛","last_name":"丁","orcid":"0009-0006-6949-1700"},{"first_name":"Zhihong","last_name":"Liu","orcid":"0000-0002-3765-8266"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bladder Cancer","Messenger RNA"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4mb5kjywkj","title":"Western Blot raw data of the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\"","description":"The raw images of WB is for the reviewers' request when reviewing the manuscript titled \"In vivo CRISPR knockout screen identifies p47 as a suppressor for HER2+ breast cancer metastasis by regulating NEMO trafficking and autophagy flux\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-04T00:26:32.621Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4mb5kjywkj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4mb5kjywkj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mingang","last_name":"Hao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5wbzddvhbx","title":"Cisplatin Damaged Gene Loci ","description":"Cisplatin is a classical example of a DNA-targeted genotoxic anticancer drug, yet the exact gene loci attacked by cisplatin as well as the relationship between these gene lesions and action of the drug remain largely unexplored. In this work, we have used a forward chemical genetics method to localize and identify cisplatin damaged gene loci on the whole human genome. By combining affinity separation, high throughput sequencing and mapping to human genome (hg38), we have mapped 14012 protein-encoding genes containing platination lesions, of which 445 genes belong to 628 known gene targets for cancer therapy reported previously. Significantly, cisplatin induces lesions on 306 protein kinase genes with high fold-enrichment during our gene loci mapping, accounting for a remarkable 59% of putative human protein kinase genes. The cisplatin-damaged protein kinase genes are involved in 260 highly associated core-signaling pathways. The data deposited into this database are the mapping results of cisplatin damaged DNA for two biological replicates.     \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-26T18:58:29.8Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5wbzddvhbx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5wbzddvhbx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fuyi","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pharmacology","Anticancer Drug"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ymn3ykkmyx","title":"A systematic CRISPR screen defines mutational mechanisms underpinning signatures caused by replication errors and endogenous DNA damage","description":"Mutational signatures are imprints of pathophysiological processes arising through tumorigenesis. We generated isogenic CRISPR–Cas9 knockouts (∆) of 43 genes in human induced pluripotent stem cells, cultured them in the absence of added DNA damage and performed whole-genome sequencing of 173 subclones. ∆OGG1, ∆UNG, ∆EXO1, ∆RNF168, ∆MLH1, ∆MSH2, ∆MSH6, ∆PMS1 and ∆PMS2 produced marked mutational signatures indicative of them being critical mitigators of endogenous DNA modifications. Detailed analyses revealed mutational mechanistic insights, including how 8-oxo-2′-deoxyguanosine elimination is sequence context specific while uracil clearance is sequence context independent. Mismatch repair (MMR) deficiency signatures are engendered by oxidative damage (C > A transversions) and differential misincorporation by replicative polymerases (T > C and C > T transitions), and we propose a reverse template slippage model for T > A transversions. ∆MLH1, ∆MSH6 and ∆MSH2 signatures were similar to each other but distinct from ∆PMS2. Finally, we developed a classifier, MMRDetect, where application to 7,695 whole-genome-sequenced cancers showed enhanced detection of MMR-deficient tumors, with implications for responsiveness to immunotherapies.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-04-19T14:39:59.262Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ymn3ykkmyx","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ymn3ykkmyx"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Serena","last_name":"Nik-Zainal","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genomics","Cancer Genetics","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":139505,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":187,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":139505,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":138763,"restricted":69,"embargoed":673},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2306},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1391},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":673},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":473},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":452},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":436},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":422},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":390},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":328},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":276}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":136854},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1908},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":186},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":137},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":79},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":57},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":43},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":903},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":857},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":527},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":363}]}}